# Ownership and required review.
#
# Replace the placeholder handles below with real GitHub teams or usernames before
# this does anything. It is inert until branch protection on `main` has "Require
# review from Code Owners" switched on — without that, this file is documentation.
#
# IMPORTANT, and easy to get wrong: when a line lists several owners, GitHub is
# satisfied by an approval from ANY ONE of them. CODEOWNERS on its own therefore
# cannot express "the backend lead AND the PO must both approve". Only one rule
# applies to a file — the last one that matches — so a second line for the same path
# does not add a second requirement. Genuinely requiring both needs a check that
# inspects who approved. See contract/README.md.

# The contract belongs to both teams. Neither may change it alone.
/contract/            @abulkheir/backend-leads @abulkheir/product-owners

# The source of truth and the record of what we assumed in its absence.
/backlog/             @abulkheir/product-owners
/brd/                 @abulkheir/product-owners
/assumptions/         @abulkheir/product-owners

# The rules the process itself runs on.
/tools/               @abulkheir/backend-leads @abulkheir/product-owners
/docs/                @abulkheir/product-owners
/.github/             @abulkheir/backend-leads @abulkheir/product-owners

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On a PERSONAL account rather than an organization, teams do not exist at all.
# Replace every @abulkheir/<team> above with the individual usernames, e.g.
#
#   /contract/   @abulkheir @second-account
#
# and switch .github/dual-approval.json to its "users" form. The dual-approval
# check supports both; CODEOWNERS on its own still cannot require BOTH of them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
